  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 340 PM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nWith the cold front departing off to our south by Sunday morning, \nsurface high pressure and drier air will fill in over the region. \nLooking broadly at the upper-levels, an omega block tries to take \nshape as two upper-level lows flank both sides of a well-amplified \nridge over the central CONUS. Central Kentucky and southern Indiana \nwill be placed just to the east of the ridge, with northerly flow \naloft persisting into the early portions of next week. Temperatures \nto close off the weekend and start the following workweek will be \ncloser to normal for this time of year, with highs ranging from the \nmid 80s to around 90. The biggest benefit from this pattern change \nwill be the increase in drier air, allowing dew points during the \novernight hours to drop into the 60s. This will help with overnight \ntemperature recovery, allowing for lows to also be in the 60s Sunday \nand Monday night.\n\nAs the workweek progresses, the previously mentioned omega blocking \npattern quickly breaks down, causing the upper ridge to move \neastward towards the Ohio Valley. While the upper-level flow will \ncontinue to be northerly over the region, lower-level and surface \nflow will shift to the south, allowing warmer air to fill back in. \nWidespread afternoon highs in the 90s will return Tuesday, lasting \nthrough the rest of the forecast period. A few weak perturbations \npassing over the region could allow for a few isolated diurnally-\ndriven showers and storms to develop, but with limited moisture and \nweak wind shear in place, chances will remain rather low through \nWednesday (<20% chance). \n\nThe end of the forecast period on Thursday becomes more uncertain as \nanother pattern shift looks to take shape. Most deterministic and \nensemble guidance picks up on a robust upper level low moving from \ncentral Canada towards the NE CONUS while a surface cold front \npasses over the Ohio Valley. The uncertainty lies in the timing and \nprogression of these features, but it does appear that this could be \nthe next chance for more appreciable precipitation across the region.",
